TypeScript is a strongly typed language that extends JavaScript by adding optional static typing, type annotations, and compile-time type checking. It provides a way to write more structured code with features like interfaces, generics, enums, tuples, async/await functions, modules, etc.

The key advantage of TypeScript compared to regular JavaScript is that it enables developers to add type information at the source level of their code which helps catch errors before they even try to execute the program. This encourages better coding practices and a more organized approach in JavaScript development.

Duck Typing or "structural subtyping" is a design pattern in object-oriented programming languages that allows objects with different data structures (or classes) to be used interchangeably within a specific context if they have similar traits or share the same interface. Essentially, an entity can be treated as another if it can perform certain tasks similarly.

TypeScript offers structural subtyping using its interfaces. By defining an interface, TypeScript allows you to specify properties, methods, constructors, and other members of an object. You can then use this interface to enforce contracts between types, ensuring your program will work as intended.

For example, consider two entities: a Person object and an Employee object:

```typescript
interface IPerson {
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

class Person implements IPerson {
  constructor(public name: string, public age: number) {}

  speak(): void {
    console.log(`My name is ${this.name}`);
  }
}

interface IEmployee extends IPerson {         // Structural Subtyping - IEmployee has a superset of IPerson's properties
  employeeID: string;                         // This property makes 'employee' different from 'person'
}

class Employee implements IEmployee {
  constructor(public name: string, public age: number, public employeeID: string) {}

  report() {
    console.log(`${this.employeeID} reports to manager`);
  }
}
```

In this code snippet, both the `Person` and `Employee` classes implement the `IPerson` interface, enabling them to have the same behavior without being exactly the same type.

This is particularly useful when dealing with objects that aren't defined explicitly. By making use of this pattern, TypeScript guarantees that any given instance having sufficient structure can participate in operations where type is not strictly enforced. 

Duck typing, combined with TypeScript's typing system, empowers developers to write expressive, safe, and flexible code while reducing the risk of runtime errors.

